      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(2)=1\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=1-(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-1)+(2)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)-(2)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=- \\frac{1}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $\u27e8- \\frac{1}{2},-1,2\u27e9.$",
